PLACES=[
# ---- New York City ----
N('octagon-nyc-lunatic-asylum-ny','The Octagon (New York City Lunatic Asylum)','Roosevelt Island (Manhattan)','nyc','inst','standing',40.76901,-73.94370,'exact','view','walk','','1835–1955',
  "The domed octagonal rotunda was the entrance to the New York City Lunatic Asylum on what was then Blackwell's Island, the city's asylum for the poor, which opened in 1841. Alexander Jackson Davis designed it, and it was built of the blue-grey stone quarried on the island; Wikipedia gives 1834 and the Register record 1835. The asylum's treatment of patients was the subject of Nellie Bly's 1887 exposé, Ten Days in a Mad-House. It became part of the Metropolitan Hospital in 1894 and closed in 1955; the rest of the asylum was pulled down, and the empty rotunda burned twice and came close to ruin. It was put on the National Register in 1972 and made a city landmark in 1976, and in April 2006 it reopened, restored, as the lobby of two new apartment buildings with 500 flats.",
  'The five-storey stone octagon under its dome, with the new apartment building beside it modelled on the old asylum. Survey photographs of 1970 show a spiral stair round an open stairwell inside; it is now a private lobby and was not checked as it is now.',
  'The last piece of the asylum Nellie Bly went into undercover.',
  '888 Main Street, Roosevelt Island. A private apartment building: look from Main Street.',
  [W('The Octagon (Roosevelt Island)'),L('National Register record: The Octagon','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/72000880')],
  brick='no',arch='Alexander Jackson Davis',archw='National Register record and Wikipedia: designed by Alexander Jackson Davis',conf='mixed'),
N('buell-hall-bloomingdale-asylum-ny','Buell Hall (Macy Villa of the Bloomingdale Asylum)','Manhattan (Morningside Heights)','nyc','inst','standing',40.80771,-73.96142,'exact','view','walk','','1885–',
  "New York Hospital's Bloomingdale Insane Asylum opened on the heights in 1821 in a Federal style brownstone building, and from 1841, when the city's poor patients went to Blackwell's Island, it took only those who could pay. Macy Villa, built in 1885 to Ralph Townsend's design as a house for well-off men among the patients and named for William H. Macy, was the last building it put up. The asylum moved to White Plains in 1889 and Columbia College bought most of its land in 1892 for its new campus. The villa is the only asylum building left and the oldest building on Columbia's campus: it was College Hall, was moved back to make room for Kent Hall and became East Hall, and since 1983 has been Buell Hall, home of La Maison Française and the Buell Center for the Study of American Architecture.",
  'A gabled brick house with white trim. Interior not checked.',
  'The one building left of the asylum Columbia was built on.',
  'On the Columbia University campus by College Walk. University property: look from outside; whether visitors may walk the campus was not checked.',
  [W('Buell Hall'),W('Bloomingdale Insane Asylum')],
  brick='?',arch='Ralph Townsend',archw='Wikipedia: designed by Ralph Townsend'),
N('willowbrook-state-school-ny','Willowbrook State School and the Willowbrook Mile','Staten Island (Willowbrook)','nyc','inst','standing',40.60300,-74.14800,'area','public','walk','','1938–1987',
  "The state drew up plans in 1938 for an institution for people with intellectual disabilities on 375 acres at Willowbrook. It was finished in 1942 but went first to the Army as Halloran General Hospital; Wikipedia's Pilgrim article says it was built to the same design as Pilgrim State Hospital. It opened for its own purpose in 1947. A 1972 exposé of its overcrowding and abuse by Geraldo Rivera, a class-action suit filed that year and the consent judgment of 1975 began the emptying of New York's large institutions; the last children left on September 17, 1987. The city took part of the land in 1989 for the College of Staten Island, which opened here in 1993 using some of the old buildings; the state's developmental disabilities office keeps the rest. The Willowbrook Mile, a memorial walking trail with 12 stations, opened on September 17, 2022, the 35th anniversary of the closing.",
  'Old institution buildings now used by the college, with the twelve stations of the Willowbrook Mile, benches and planting. Two stations on the state\'s part are the Baby Unit and the Connelly Center. Which buildings are original was not checked.',
  'A college campus that was the institution that ended institutions.',
  'The College of Staten Island campus and the state\'s land beside it along Forest Hill Road. The college says its stations (1 to 6, 11 and 12) are open dawn to dusk every day the college is open, and the gate officers give maps and parking information (a day permit is sold online); the state\'s stations (7 to 10) are open weekdays dawn to dusk, with parking by station 10 on Forest Hill Road at D Street, and 7 and 8 are shut at weekends.',
  [W('Willowbrook State School'),L('College of Staten Island: Willowbrook Mile','https://csi.cuny.edu/about-csi/president-leadership/administration/office-vp-economic-development-and-community-partnerships/reporting-units-and-initiatives/willowbrook-mile'),L('College of Staten Island: Willowbrook Mile, know before you go','https://www.csi.cuny.edu/about-csi/president-leadership/administration/office-vp-economic-development-and-community-partnerships/reporting-units-and-initiatives/willowbrook-mile/know-you-go')],
  brick='?',hours='College stations dawn to dusk when the college is open; state stations weekdays dawn to dusk'),
N('queens-county-farm-creedmoor-ny','Queens County Farm Museum (Creedmoor State Hospital farm)','Queens (Glen Oaks)','nyc','landmark','standing',40.74837,-73.72017,'exact','open','walk','','1772; hospital farm 1926–1975',
  "The Adriance family farmed here from 1697 to 1808, and their farmhouse of 1772 (Wikipedia also gives 1750 for the house) still stands. By 1900 it was among the largest and most valuable farms in Queens. In 1926 Creedmoor State Hospital bought it and worked it as the hospital farm, for occupational therapy, to stock its kitchens and to grow plants for the grounds; Creedmoor had opened next door in 1912 as the farm colony of Brooklyn State Hospital. A state law of 1975 handed the farm from the hospital to New York City Parks for a museum. It is the largest stretch of undisturbed farmland left in the city, and still a working farm.",
  'The restored Adriance Farmhouse (a city landmark, on the National Register since 1979), farm buildings, fields, livestock and old farm machinery. Guided tours of the farmhouse are given; Creedmoor\'s own hospital, still a working state hospital, is not open to visitors.',
  'The asylum farm that became the city\'s last big farm.',
  '73-50 Little Neck Parkway, Glen Oaks. Run by Queens Farm on NYC Parks land. Its visit page (read November 2026): grounds open daily 10 to 5 all year, closed New Year\'s Day, Labor Day, Thanksgiving and Christmas, free except on ticketed event days; free street parking on Little Neck Parkway, and the lot on site opens only for events.',
  [W('Queens County Farm Museum'),W('Creedmoor Psychiatric Center'),L('Queens Farm: plan your visit','https://www.queensfarm.org/visit/')],
  brick='?',hours='Daily 10–5, year round; closed New Year\'s Day, Labor Day, Thanksgiving, Christmas',conf='mixed'),
# ---- Mid-Hudson and Rockland ----
N('matteawan-state-hospital-ny','Matteawan State Hospital for the Criminally Insane','Beacon (Town of Fishkill)','midh','inst','standing',41.52221,-73.95057,'area','view','drive','','1892–1977',
  "In 1886 a state commission advised buying the 246-acre Dates Farm at Matteawan for an asylum for insane criminals, and Isaac G. Perry, who finished the State Capitol, designed the main building for 550 patients. The asylum moved here from Auburn in April 1892 with 261 patients and was named Matteawan State Hospital the next year; it took men not yet convicted and women, while men who fell ill in prison went to Dannemora. A farm of up to 700 acres fed it until the mid-1960s, and by 1949, built for 550, it held almost 1,500 men and 250 women. It closed in 1977, when the forensic service moved to Marcy, and its buildings became part of Fishkill Correctional Facility. Hudson Valley Ruins found most of Perry's main building in prison use in 2007, but the central wing of its west front empty, with no plan to keep it.",
  'The long main building inside the prison grounds, seen only from a distance. The hospital cemetery, with nearly 1,000 patients\' graves, is west of Beacon High School on Matteawan Road; who may visit it was not checked.',
  '',
  'Fishkill Correctional Facility (state Department of Corrections), 18 Strack Drive, Beacon. '+PRISON,
  [W('Matteawan State Hospital for the Criminally Insane'),L('Hudson Valley Ruins: Matteawan State Hospital','https://www.hudsonvalleyruins.org/yasinsac/dutchess/matteawan1.html')],
  brick='?',arch='Isaac G. Perry',archw='Wikipedia and Hudson Valley Ruins: Isaac Perry designed the main hospital building'),
N('middletown-state-homeopathic-hospital-ny','Middletown State Homeopathic Hospital','Middletown','midh','inst','standing',41.45200,-74.44200,'area','view','drive','','1874–2006',
  "The legislature voted on April 28, 1870 for a state hospital at Middletown run on homeopathic lines, and it opened on April 20, 1874 with 69 patients, the first purely homeopathic mental hospital in the country. Superintendent Seldon H. Talcott (1877–1902) used work, art and sport as treatment, and from 1889 the hospital fielded a baseball team, the Asylums, that played the New York Giants. By the early 1900s it had about 100 buildings, and nearly 4,000 patients at its peak in the 1960s. It closed in 2006. The Main Building of 1874 no longer exists, and other buildings were lost to fire and demolition; a fire gutted one in 2015. Fei Tian College has bought and renovated many of the rest since 2018, and in July 2024 the city bought more than 33 acres of state land there for one dollar, among them Tuckerman Hall and the Kleiner Center of 1968.",
  'A large campus of old hospital buildings along Seward Avenue and Dorothea Dix Drive, some renovated by the college (Woodman Hall among them), others still empty, Tuckerman Hall among them. The Main Building is gone.',
  'The asylum that played baseball against the Giants.',
  'The grounds are now the college\'s, the city\'s and other owners\'; the empty buildings have drawn trespassers and police calls. Look from the public streets only.',
  [W('Middletown State Hospital'),L('WAMC: Middletown acquires state-owned portion of abandoned psych center (2024)','https://www.wamc.org/news/2024-07-11/middletown-acquires-state-owned-portion-of-abandoned-psych-center'),L('Untapped New York: the abandoned Middletown State Homeopathic Hospital (2019)','https://untappedcities.com/2019/12/26/exploring-the-abandoned-middletown-state-homeopathic-hospital-in-new-york/'),L(AP+'Middletown State Hospital','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/Middletown_State_Hospital')],
  brick='?'),
N('letchworth-village-ny','Letchworth Village','Thiells','midh','inst','ruins',41.21479,-74.02368,'area','view','drive','','1911–1996',
  "A state institution \"for the segregation of the epileptic and feeble-minded\", opened in 1911 on 2,362 acres and named for the reformer William Pryor Letchworth. It was planned as a farming village of small fieldstone buildings modelled on Monticello, none over two storeys or holding more than 70 people, spaced well apart, each with its own playground; at its peak there were more than 130 buildings. Reports of neglect began in the 1920s, overcrowding followed, and a 1972 exposé by Geraldo Rivera brought it national attention. It closed in 1996. The Town of Stony Point bought part of the land in 1999 and the Town of Haverstraw part in 2001; almost 30 abandoned buildings stand derelict, some vandalised or burned, and both towns have put forward plans for housing and recreation.",
  'Neoclassical fieldstone dormitories, dining halls and staff houses, many roofless or burned, spread through the woods and fields. The administration building\'s roof carries the name of Dr. Charles Little, the first superintendent. Some of the old playground basketball courts are still kept up for public use (2021).',
  'A model village in fieldstone, left to the woods.',
  'Owned by the Towns of Haverstraw and Stony Point; part of the Stony Point land is the Patriot Hills Golf Course and Veterans Memorial Park off Willow Grove Road. Some of the old buildings hold asbestos and lead, and many are derelict: look from the roads and the town parks only, and do not go in.',
  [W('Letchworth Village'),L('Rockland County Business Journal: two development proposals for Letchworth Village (2022)','https://rcbizjournal.com/2022/10/18/a-tale-of-two-letchworths-both-propose-housing-but-the-devil-is-in-the-details/')],
  brick='no'),
N('letchworth-village-old-cemetery-ny','Letchworth Village Old Cemetery','Stony Point','midh','burial','standing',41.21641,-74.04924,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1914–1967',
  "From 1914 to 1967 residents of Letchworth Village who died there and were not claimed by their families were buried in a clearing in the woods off Call Hollow Road, about a mile from the institution and hidden from the road by trees. Most graves have only a T-shaped steel marker with a number; families could put up a headstone at their own cost, but most graves still have only the steel marker. A memorial stone with a plaque of the names was set at the entrance in 2007. A new cemetery opened near Thiells Mount Ivy Road in 1967, and the old graves were not moved.",
  'Rows of numbered steel T-markers in a forest clearing, a few headstones, and the 2007 memorial stone at the entrance. Wikipedia says few markers have been vandalised.',
  '',
  'Wikipedia says the Town of Stony Point owns and keeps it with volunteers from the community (OpenStreetMap puts the clearing in the Town of Haverstraw). Off Call Hollow Road; be quiet and respectful, as at any cemetery, and visit in daylight.',
  [W('Letchworth Village')],
  brick='no',apart='letchworth-village-ny: the burial ground is a separate clearing about a mile from the institution\'s buildings'),
# ---- North Country ----
N('st-lawrence-state-hospital-ny','St. Lawrence State Hospital','Ogdensburg','nny','inst','standing',44.72528,-75.44583,'area','view','drive','','1890–1983',
  "In 1886 a state commission, William P. Letchworth among its members, chose Airy Point on the St. Lawrence River for a new state asylum for the insane. Building began in 1888 to the designs of Isaac G. Perry, the state architect, on the cottage plan, as three groups of small buildings so that patients could be housed apart by illness; it opened on December 9, 1890. It ran its own farms, fire and police, post office and theatre, and a nursing school from 1890 until 1981. The old hospital closed in 1983 (Michael Kleen) and its buildings were adapted for Ogdensburg Correctional Facility from 1982 (Wikipedia), which closed in 2022; a smaller St. Lawrence Psychiatric Center still works on part of the grounds. The state found the campus eligible for the National Register as a historic district, yet began pulling down empty buildings in 2023: the ten-building Letchworth complex (about 135,000 square feet) first, then seven service buildings built between 1900 and 1970.",
  'A large riverside campus of old hospital buildings, some still in use and many empty. Which of Perry\'s buildings still stand after the demolitions was not checked.',
  '',
  'State land: the active St. Lawrence Psychiatric Center at 1 Chimney Point Drive and the closed prison. Look from the public roads only; do not enter the grounds or the empty buildings.',
  [W('Ogdensburg Correctional Facility'),L('Michael Kleen: St. Lawrence State Hospital (2018)','https://michaelkleen.com/2018/10/10/st-lawrence-state-hospital-in-ogdensburg-new-york/'),L('DEC notice: Letchworth complex demolition, phases 1 and 2 (2023)','https://dec.ny.gov/news/environmental-notice-bulletin/2023-09-27/seqr/city-of-ogdensburg-st-lawrence-psychiatric-center/letchworth-complex-demolition-phases-1-2'),L('DASNY: phase 3 demolition finding (2024)','https://www.dasny.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/StLawrence%20Phase%203%20demo%20Neg%20Dec%202024-04-11.pdf'),L(AP+'St. Lawrence State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/St._Lawrence_State_Hospital')],
  brick='?',arch='Isaac G. Perry',archw='Michael Kleen and Asylum Projects: designed by the state architect I. G. Perry',conf='mixed'),
# ---- Western New York ----
N('gowanda-state-hospital-ny','Gowanda State Homeopathic Hospital','Collins (Gowanda)','wny','inst','standing',42.48833,-78.93694,'area','view','drive','','1898–2021',
  "Erie County bought the 500-acre Taylor Tract on Taylor Hollow Road in 1894 for a homeopathic state hospital, and the land passed to the state the same year. The Buffalo firm Esenwein & Johnson designed it on the echelon plan and worked there from 1896 to 1912; the first patients came on August 9, 1898. As the Gowanda Psychiatric Center wound down, 40 per cent of the land became Collins Correctional Facility in 1982 and the rest Gowanda Correctional Facility in 1994, at one time the state's second-largest prison. Gowanda prison closed in 2021 (the closing was set for the end of March). The state's 2025 study of the empty 68-acre site found its 26 buildings in good condition and five of them (32, 61, 65, 66 and 67) eligible for the National Register.",
  'Old hospital buildings among the prison housing of the closed facility; Collins Correctional Facility, still open, is next door behind a fence.',
  '',
  'State land of the prisons department and the Office of Mental Health, on South Road between Taylor Hollow Road and Route 62. Collins next door is an active prison; look from the public roads only.',
  [W('Gowanda State Hospital'),L('Empire State Development: Gowanda site feasibility study (2025)','https://esd.ny.gov/sites/default/files/media/document/Gowanda-Feasibility-Study-080625.pdf'),L('State Senate: officials slam decision to close Gowanda Correctional Facility (2020)','https://www.nysenate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2020/george-borrello/state-local-officials-slam-decision-close-gowanda'),L(AP+'Gowanda State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/Gowanda_State_Hospital')],
  brick='?',arch='Esenwein & Johnson',archw='Asylum Projects: Esenwein & Johnson of Buffalo, 1896 to 1912'),
N('craig-colony-sonyea-ny','Craig Colony for Epileptics','Sonyea','wny','inst','standing',42.68216,-77.83613,'area','view','drive','','1896–1988',
  "Shakers who moved here from Sodus lived at Sonyea until 1894, when the state bought their 1,900 acres for a colony for people with epilepsy on the model of Bielefeld in Germany, named for Oscar Craig of the State Board of Charities. William Pryor Letchworth and Dr. Frederick Peterson pressed for the site; George Metzger of Buffalo was the architect in 1896, and the Olmsted Brothers laid out the grounds. The first residents came in 1896, the old Shaker buildings were put to use, and the colony grew to more than 2,000 people in the 1960s. Renamed several times, it became Craig Developmental Center in 1969, and the state turned it into Groveland Correctional Facility in the 1980s; the last residents left in November 1988.",
  'Several Shaker buildings still stand inside the prison, among them the large brick Letchworth building (Wikipedia); the Shakers\' House of Elders burned about 1946. Sonyea State Forest takes in the colony\'s old woods.',
  '',
  'Groveland Correctional Facility (state Department of Corrections) on Sonyea Road. '+PRISON,
  [W('Craig Colony for Epileptics'),W('Groveland Shaker Village'),W('Groveland Correctional Facility'),L('Livingston County Historian: Craig Colony','https://livingstoncountyny.gov/1703/Craig-Colony')],
  brick='?',arch='George Metzger',archw='Wikipedia: George Metzger of Buffalo was the architect in 1896'),
N('brigham-hall-canandaigua-ny','Brigham Hall','Canandaigua','wny','inst','standing',42.87893,-77.29038,'site','view','drive','','1855–1961',
  "A private asylum founded by Dr. Cook with Robert Cook, Dr. John B. Chapin and Willard G. Wayne, which opened on October 3, 1855 in the old Clark mansion. The main building of about 1855 has a centre of brick and fieldstone, one and a half to two storeys, between two-storey brick wings; Wikipedia calls it Gothic Revival and the Register record Greek Revival. Around it stand nine more contributing structures: Heritage House and two residence units of the early 1900s, a recreation building of 1908 to 1924, a frame storage building, a paint shop, a cistern and a gazebo. The asylum closed in 1960 or 1961 (Asylum Projects gives both), a wing became the Grove Home nursing home until 1979, and the main buildings were then made into condominiums. On the National Register since 1984.",
  'The brick and fieldstone main building and its brick wings, with the older outbuildings round it. Private homes: interiors not open.',
  '',
  '229 Bristol Street. Brigham Hall Condominiums and other private homes: look from Bristol Street.',
  [W('Brigham Hall'),L('National Register record: Brigham Hall','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/84002827'),L(AP+'Brigham Hall','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/Brigham_Hall')],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
]
